WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 273: J. Robert Oppenheimer: The Manhattan Project, Trinity, and the Prometheus of the Atomic Age

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:15.360
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series explores the complex moral and scientific drama of the atomic age.

4
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:22.800
[Rebecca] The visionary physicist who built the atomic bomb and spent the rest of his life grappling with its existential consequences.

5
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.760
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary story of J.

6
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.760
[Rebecca] Robert Oppenheimer.

7
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:44.960
[George] Born in April 1904 to wealthy, cultured German Jewish textile merchants in New York City, Julius Robert Oppenheimer grew up in an apartment on Riverside Drive lined with original paintings by Van Gogh, Cezanne, and Rembrandt.

8
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:57.760
[George] An intensely introverted prodigy with an omnivorous intellect, young Robert collected rare minerals, read classical Greek and French poetry, and mastered multiple foreign languages before entering Harvard.

9
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:07.840
[George] Completing his undergraduate degree in chemistry summa cum laude in just three years, Oppenheimer traveled to Europe during the revolutionary birth of quantum mechanics.

10
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:24.880
[George] At the Cavendish Laboratory in Cambridge and Max Born's renowned institute in Gottingen, Germany, Oppenheimer worked alongside Paul Durack, Werner Heisenberg, and Wolfgang Pauly, publishing landmark papers on the quantum behavior of molecules and continuous spectra.

11
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:40.960
[George] Returning to the United States in 1929 with an ethereal, charismatic presence and a chainsmoking habit, Oppenheimer established the premier school of theoretical physics in America, splitting his time between the University of California at Berkeley and Caltech.

12
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:57.520
[George] His students hung on his every whisper, imitating his gestures and dry wit, while his pioneering theoretical work predicted the existence of positrons, neutron stars, and the gravitational collapse of black holes a decade before astronomical discovery confirmed them.

13
00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:09.240
[George] His undergraduate years at Harvard revealed a polymathic genius who audited graduate courses in Chinese, Sanskrit, and mathematics while writing avant garde sonnets for the Harvard Advocate.

14
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:23.000
[George] His groundbreaking work at Gottingen with Max Bourne formulated the Born Oppenheimer approximation, a cornerstone of molecular quantum physics that allowed physicists to treat the motions of atomic nuclei and electrons separately.

15
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:38.840
[George] At Berkeley, his lively seminars attracted a passionate cadre of brilliant young disciples who gathered at his hilltop home on Eagle Hill to discuss general relativity, quantum electrodynamics, and Marxist economic theories over fiery Indonesian cuisine.

16
00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:42.360
[George] In the autumn of 1942, Colonel Leslie R.

17
00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:54.680
[George] Groves of the Army Corps of Engineers was appointed military director of the top secret Manhattan Project, tasked with designing an atomic weapon before Nazi Germany could unravel the secrets of nuclear fission.

18
00:02:54.680 --> 00:03:02.200
[George] Groves surprised the military establishment by choosing the 38-year-old Oppenheimer as scientific director of Project Y.

19
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:11.480
[George] Oppenheimer had never won a Nobel Prize, possessed no administrative experience, and carried left-wing political associations from the 1930s.

20
00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:22.600
[George] Yet Groves recognized Oppenheimer's rare, luminous genius, an extraordinary ability to synthesize disparate scientific fields and inspire creative prima donnas.

21
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:36.520
[George] Oppenheimer insisted on establishing the secret laboratory atop the remote Paja Rito Plateau at Los Alamos, New Mexico, an ancient mesa surrounded by pine covered canyons where he had spent boyhood summers riding horses.

22
00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:58.080
[George] Over the next two years, the Sleepy Ranch School was transformed into an armed, barbed wire citadel of 4,000 civilian scientists, engineers, and military personnel, gathering world-renowned luminaries including Enrico Fermi, Hans Beth, Edward Teller, and Richard Feynman under the clandestine identity of Site Y.

23
00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:13.120
[George] General Groves appreciated Oppenheimer's uncanny ability to articulate complex physical principles with concise, crystal clarity, cutting through bureaucratic red tape to establish immediate authority over military and civilian staff alike.

24
00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:23.760
[George] Oppenheimer personally selected the isolated Lowe's Alamos site because its sheer sandstone cliffs and expansive horizons evoked a profound sense of monastic purpose.

25
00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:32.000
[George] Believing that scientists isolated from the outside world would forge an esprit de corps essential for solving impossible technical riddles.

26
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:37.360
[George] The Lowe's Alamos laboratory operated with an astonishing lack of academic protocol.

27
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:46.960
[George] Nobel laureates worked in denim jackets alongside young graduate students in prefabricated wooden barracks, united under Oppenheimer's magnetic leadership.

28
00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:51.840
[George] The theoretical and engineering obstacles facing Lowe's Alamos were colossal.

29
00:04:51.840 --> 00:05:11.200
[George] While the gun type assembly method proved workable for uranium-235 in the weapon codenamed Little Boy, reactorbred plutonium-239 from Hanford was found to contain trace isotopes of plutonium-240, whose high spontaneous fission rate threatened to cause a premature predetonation or fizzle.

30
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:25.280
[George] Oppenheimer made the daring organizational decision to completely reorganize the laboratory overnight, channeling hundreds of scientists into the radical, unproven implosion method championed by Seth Nedermeier and George Kisciakowski.

31
00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:37.880
[George] Implosion required a hollow subcritical sphere of plutonium to be compressed symmetrically inward to supercritical density within millionths of a second by high explosive lens charges, working 18-hour days.

32
00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:56.920
[George] His weight dropping below 115 pounds under crushing administrative and moral pressure, Oppenhamer mediated intense scientific rivalries, coordinated chemical, metallurgical and electronic breakthroughs, and maintained high morale through his quiet authority and intellectual brilliance.

33
00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:14.280
[George] The discovery by Emilio Segar in June 1944 that reactorbred plutonium was contaminated by the isotope Plutonium-240 precipitated the greatest crisis in the history of the Manhattan Project, rendering the artillery-style, thin-man gun design utterly obsolete.

34
00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:30.600
[George] Oppenhamer demonstrated supreme executive decisiveness by sweeping away the existing laboratory hierarchy in a single weekend, reassigning hundreds of theoretical and experimental physicists to the newly created G-division and X division to master explosive implosion.

35
00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:38.440
[George] George Kishiakowski's team manufactured thousands of precision-machined explosive lenses of composition B and Baritol.

36
00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:48.280
[George] Detonated by fast-acting spark gap switches to generate an inward-propagating spherical shockwave that compressed the solid plutonium core with mathematical symmetry.

37
00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:58.440
[George] In the desolate Jornada del Muerto Desert near Alamogordo, New Mexico, a 100-foot steel tower held the test device codenamed the Gadget.

38
00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:13.480
[George] In the pre-dawn darkness of July 16, 1945, amid torrential rain and lightning, Oppenhamer stood inside the concrete control bunker 10,000 yards away, nervously whispering to himself as the countdown reached zero.

39
00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:32.080
[George] At exactly 5.29 in the morning, the desert exploded into a blinding flash brighter than a thousand suns, vaporizing the steel tower, fusing the desert sand into green radioactive trinitite glass, and sending a boiling purple mushroom cloud 40,000 feet into the stratosphere.

40
00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:35.680
[George] The shockwave knocked observers off their feet miles away.

41
00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:48.960
[George] Watching the terrifying cosmic fury unleashed by the fission of a grapefruit-sized core of plutonium, Oppenhamer later recalled lines from the sacred Hindu scripture, the Bhagavad Gita, Now I am become death.

42
00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:50.960
[George] The destroyer of worlds.

43
00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:57.520
[George] Physics had known sin, and the human race had crossed into a perilous new epoch of existential power.

44
00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:04.240
[George] In the final hours before the test, while Oppenhamer paced nervously inside the wooden shack housing the gadget.

45
00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:15.280
[George] Physicist Enrico Fermi offered grim gallows humor by offering to take side bets on whether the nuclear detonation would ignite the Earth's atmosphere and destroy all life on the planet.

46
00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:29.440
[George] When the blinding cosmic brilliance erupted across the desert floor, turning the night sky into noon and searing the eyes of observers through dark welder's glass, the profound silence of the initial seconds felt like the opening of eternity.

47
00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:41.920
[George] As the thunderous shockwave rattled the concrete observation bunker six miles away, Test director Kenneth Bainbridge turned to Oppenhamer and uttered the immortal, unvarnished verdict of the scientific team.

48
00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:44.400
[George] Now we are all sons of bitches.

49
00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] Three weeks later, on August 6 and August 9, 1945, Little Boy and Fat Man were detonated over Hiroshima and Nagasaki, killing over 100,000 civilians instantly and forcing Imperial Japan's unconditional surrender.

50
00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:14.520
[George] When Oppenhamer addressed a jubilant crowd of Lowe's Alamos staff in the auditorium, he clapped his hands over his head like a winning prizefighter, expressing only regret that the weapon had not been completed in time to use against Nazi Germany.

51
00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:24.520
[George] But as graphic photographs and reports of catastrophic radiation sickness arrived from the ash heaps of Japan, Oppenhamer was overcome with deep moral sorrow.

52
00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:30.280
[George] Visiting the Oval Office in October 1945, Oppenhamer confessed to President Harry S.

53
00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:31.800
[George] Truman, Mr.

54
00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:35.320
[George] President, I feel I have blood on my hands.

55
00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:39.960
[George] An infuriated Truman offered him a handkerchief and later barked to his aides.

56
00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.680
[George] Never bring that damn Cretan in here again.

57
00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:44.600
[George] He didn't drop the bomb.

58
00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:58.120
[George] I did, while Oppenhamer had actively participated on the scientific advisory panel that recommended the unheralded atomic bombardment of populated Japanese industrial centers to shock Japan into immediate surrender.

59
00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:04.360
[George] The horrifying human devastation of Hiroshima and Nagasaki transformed his conscience forever.

60
00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:23.000
[George] Witnessing the dawn of nuclear proliferation, Oppenheimer became the chief intellectual architect of the 1946 Aixen Lilienthal report, proposing a radical international authority to own and control all worldwide deposits of uranium and thorium to prevent a suicidal global arms race.

61
00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:34.680
[George] When the Truman administration rejected this visionary plan in favor of the American dominated Baruch Plan, Oppenheimer recognized that the hope of international nuclear sanity was stillborn.

62
00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:44.280
[George] Appointed chairman of the General Advisory Committee of the newly created Atomic Energy Commission, Oppenheimer became the nation's most respected public scientist.

63
00:10:44.480 --> 00:11:01.600
[George] In 1949, when the Soviet Union unexpectedly detonated its own atomic device, Edward Teller and Ernest Lawrence championed an emergency crash program to develop the hydrogen superbomb, a thermonuclear weapon thousands of times more destructive than fission bombs.

64
00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:14.080
[George] Oppenheimer and his fellow advisory committee members unanimously opposed the superbomb on strategic and moral grounds, warning that a weapon of genocide would only escalate an unwinnable nuclear arms race.

65
00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:22.400
[George] Oppenheimer's principled stance earned him ruthless political enemies, notably AEC Chairman Louis Strauss and FBI Director J.

66
00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:23.760
[George] Edgar Hoover.

67
00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:39.680
[George] In December 1953, at the height of McCarthyite anti-communist hysteria, Strauss suspended Oppenheimer's security clearance, subjecting him to a grueling, four-week kangaroo hearing before the AEC personnel security board in 1954.

68
00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:48.320
[George] His private life and past political opinions were shredded by prosecutor Roger Robb, culminating in the revocation of his security clearance.

69
00:11:48.320 --> 00:12:05.360
[George] Oppenheimer's moral opposition to Edward Teller's thermonuclear super ignited bitter animosity within the United States Air Force and the emerging military-industrial complex, who viewed nuclear dominance as the sole guarantee of national security against Soviet expansionism.

70
00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:12.160
[George] The secret AEC security hearings of April 1954 were an orchestrated political execution.

71
00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:20.880
[George] Special prosecutor Roger Robb was granted unauthorized access to classified FBI wiretaps and Oppenheimer's attorney-client communications.

72
00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:27.680
[George] While Oppenheimer's defense counsel was denied security clearance to examine the government's secret evidence.

73
00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:42.360
[George] Despite passionate defense testimony from George Kennan, Vannevar Bush and Hans Beth, the board voted two to one to strip Oppenheimer of his security clearance, exiling the architect of the atomic bomb from the halls of national power.

74
00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:58.360
[George] Stripped of his government role, Oppenheimer spent his remaining years in quiet reflection as director of the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, New Jersey, nurturing world-class minds including Albert Einstein, John von Neumann, and Freeman Dyson.

75
00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:06.840
[George] He traveled internationally, lecturing on the moral responsibilities of science and the perils of technological hubris in a nuclear world.

76
00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:13.640
[George] In December 1963, in a gesture of long-delayed national restitution, President Lyndon B.

77
00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:19.960
[George] Johnson presented Oppenheimer with the nation's highest nuclear honor, the Enrico Fermi Award.

78
00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:25.240
[George] Smiling faintly, Oppenheimer remarked, I think it is just possible, Mr.

79
00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:30.840
[George] President, that it has taken some charity and some courage for you to make this award today.

80
00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:38.680
[George] Passing away from throat cancer in February 1967 at age 62, his ashes were scattered into the sea off St.

81
00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:39.320
[George] John.

82
00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:52.280
[George] In December 2022, 55 years after his death, the United States Department of Energy officially vacated the 1954 security decision, vindicating his loyalty and cementing J.

83
00:13:52.600 --> 00:14:01.480
[George] Robert Oppenheimer's legacy as the American Prometheus who brought heavenly fire to earth and bore the agonizing consequences for all mankind.

84
00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:16.240
[George] During his long exile in Princeton, Oppenheimer found deep solace in literature, classical philosophy, and the quiet camaraderie of Albert Einstein, with whom he shared daily walks across the wooded grounds of the Institute for Advanced Study.

85
00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:34.960
[George] When the Kennedy and Johnson administrations sought to heal the scars of the McCarthy era by conferring the Enrico Fermi Award in 1963, Oppenheimer accepted the honor with serene grace, recognizing that the nation was acknowledging its own moral failure rather than merely his personal vindication.

86
00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:39.520
[George] His tragic life trajectory mirrored the ancient Greek myth of Prometheus.

87
00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:51.120
[George] He had stolen the divine fire of the gods and gifted it to mortals, only to be chained to a rock of suspicion, bearing the agonizing moral burden of humanity's perilous atomic future.

88
00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:08.880
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with the heroic medical story of Dr.

89
00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:11.840
[Rebecca] Jonas Salk and the conquering of Polia.

90
00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:15.440
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

